{"parentUuid":"6805dff2-7cdd-41df-bf84-d84fa3364601","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"91d163fa-4c33-4181-a871-4269cd8b22ce","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Socket is closed","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RwsUeN44e8mnRJAuoBH9Pv"}]},"uuid":"f401a9f4-709c-4133-9868-823f3cf34c06","timestamp":"2026-11-08T23:13:44.884Z","toolUseResult":"Error: Socket is closed","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"1260c233-a71f-493d-91fd-3dca7595e035","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","root":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","branch":"feat/desktop-tun","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":2,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":2},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},{"name":"upstream","host":"github.com","remote":"chen08209/FlClash","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6805dff2-7cdd-41df-bf84-d84fa3364601","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","sessionId":"1fd4ae39-09c4-4cf0-9bfe-fc8abaa281eb","version":"2.1.317","gitBranch":"feat/desktop-tun","slug":"deep-singing-taco"}
{"parentUuid":"22aa8bde-937f-41f9-8ca1-85dd3324a965","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"91d163fa-4c33-4181-a871-4269cd8b22ce","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016p2SVzyq1E5LoU9r1Fe7Ws","type":"tool_result","content":"## Architecture: Russia-whitelist-bypass\n\n**Russian host (Relay / Portal)**\n- **Provider:** The README names Yandex Cloud and VK Cloud, and lists Selectel as an example. The relay needs a Russian IP that appears on the whitelist. The page doesn't explain how to verify that.\n- **Software:** Xray-core, run as the systemd service `whitelist-bypass-relay`.\n- **Inbound:** VLESS + REALITY on TCP/443. It accepts client connections and the reverse-bridge connection from the foreign node.\n- **Firewall:** UFW allows only SSH and TCP/443.\n- **Deployment:** `scripts/deploy-relay.sh` generates the REALITY keys and the `HOP_UUID` tunnel token, then prints the credentials for the foreign node and a client VLESS link.\n\n**Foreign host (Exit / Reverse Bridge)**\n- **Location:** Examples given are Poland, Germany, Finland, and the Netherlands.\n- **Software:** Xray-core, run as the systemd service `whitelist-bypass-foreign`.\n- **Role:** It dials out to the Russian relay and keeps a persistent REALITY tunnel open. It accepts no inbound connections on 443, so port 443 stays free for other services such as x-ui or Nginx.\n- **Outbound:** Direct freedom to the open internet.\n- **Deployment:** `scripts/deploy-foreign.sh` takes `RUSSIAN_IP`, `RELAY_REALITY_PUBLIC_KEY`, `RELAY_SHORT_ID`, and `HOP_UUID` from the relay step.\n\n**Traffic path**\n1. The phone's VLESS client connects to the relay on TCP/443 and presents a TLS handshake that looks like `ya.ru`.\n2. The foreign node has already opened a reverse REALITY tunnel into Russia. Because the connection starts from abroad, the README argues it avoids outbound blocking.\n3. The relay forwards client traffic into that reverse tunnel.\n4. The foreign node sends the traffic to the internet from its own IP.\n\n**Protocols:** VLESS, REALITY, TCP/443, and XTLS-Vision, which the README cites for low overhead.\n\n**SNI choice:** `ya.ru` is used both as the client SNI and as the REALITY masquerade target on the relay. The stated reason is that DPI sees what looks like a normal HTTPS request to a whitelisted Russian service.\n\n**Limitations and open points**\n- **Single relay:** All clients depend on one Russian VPS. If it is blocked or its IP falls off the whitelist, every client is affected.\n- **Dependence on ya.ru:** The masquerade relies on that domain remaining whitelisted and unblocked.\n- **OS support:** Only Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04 are listed.\n- **Unverified claims:** The README asserts stability and high speed but gives no measurements. The repository also has 0 stars, 0 forks, and only 2 commits.\n- **Missing details:** The docs (ARCHITECTURE, TROUBLESHOOTING) are referenced but their contents aren't in the page.\n\n**Last update:** The page shows no date for the last change to the repository. It only shows \"2 Commits\" on the main branch and a copyright of 2026. The actual last-update date isn't available from this content."}]},"uuid":"c460dea6-2dbf-4290-a084-921d3a0bd7e2","timestamp":"2026-11-08T23:13:55.698Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":308387,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## Architecture: Russia-whitelist-bypass\n\n**Russian host (Relay / Portal)**\n- **Provider:** The README names Yandex Cloud and VK Cloud, and lists Selectel as an example. The relay needs a Russian IP that appears on the whitelist. The page doesn't explain how to verify that.\n- **Software:** Xray-core, run as the systemd service `whitelist-bypass-relay`.\n- **Inbound:** VLESS + REALITY on TCP/443. It accepts client connections and the reverse-bridge connection from the foreign node.\n- **Firewall:** UFW allows only SSH and TCP/443.\n- **Deployment:** `scripts/deploy-relay.sh` generates the REALITY keys and the `HOP_UUID` tunnel token, then prints the credentials for the foreign node and a client VLESS link.\n\n**Foreign host (Exit / Reverse Bridge)**\n- **Location:** Examples given are Poland, Germany, Finland, and the Netherlands.\n- **Software:** Xray-core, run as the systemd service `whitelist-bypass-foreign`.\n- **Role:** It dials out to the Russian relay and keeps a persistent REALITY tunnel open. It accepts no inbound connections on 443, so port 443 stays free for other services such as x-ui or Nginx.\n- **Outbound:** Direct freedom to the open internet.\n- **Deployment:** `scripts/deploy-foreign.sh` takes `RUSSIAN_IP`, `RELAY_REALITY_PUBLIC_KEY`, `RELAY_SHORT_ID`, and `HOP_UUID` from the relay step.\n\n**Traffic path**\n1. The phone's VLESS client connects to the relay on TCP/443 and presents a TLS handshake that looks like `ya.ru`.\n2. The foreign node has already opened a reverse REALITY tunnel into Russia. Because the connection starts from abroad, the README argues it avoids outbound blocking.\n3. The relay forwards client traffic into that reverse tunnel.\n4. The foreign node sends the traffic to the internet from its own IP.\n\n**Protocols:** VLESS, REALITY, TCP/443, and XTLS-Vision, which the README cites for low overhead.\n\n**SNI choice:** `ya.ru` is used both as the client SNI and as the REALITY masquerade target on the relay. The stated reason is that DPI sees what looks like a normal HTTPS request to a whitelisted Russian service.\n\n**Limitations and open points**\n- **Single relay:** All clients depend on one Russian VPS. If it is blocked or its IP falls off the whitelist, every client is affected.\n- **Dependence on ya.ru:** The masquerade relies on that domain remaining whitelisted and unblocked.\n- **OS support:** Only Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04 are listed.\n- **Unverified claims:** The README asserts stability and high speed but gives no measurements. The repository also has 0 stars, 0 forks, and only 2 commits.\n- **Missing details:** The docs (ARCHITECTURE, TROUBLESHOOTING) are referenced but their contents aren't in the page.\n\n**Last update:** The page shows no date for the last change to the repository. It only shows \"2 Commits\" on the main branch and a copyright of 2026. The actual last-update date isn't available from this content.","durationMs":11534,"url":"https://github.com/trymyfear/Russia-whitelist-bypass"},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"1260c233-a71f-493d-91fd-3dca7595e035","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","root":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","branch":"feat/desktop-tun","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":2,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":2},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},{"name":"upstream","host":"github.com","remote":"chen08209/FlClash","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"22aa8bde-937f-41f9-8ca1-85dd3324a965","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","sessionId":"1fd4ae39-09c4-4cf0-9bfe-fc8abaa281eb","version":"2.1.317","gitBranch":"feat/desktop-tun","slug":"deep-singing-taco"}
{"parentUuid":"c6c30ecc-a993-4f47-9044-ce7c17ad4cb0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"91d163fa-4c33-4181-a871-4269cd8b22ce","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01647zFr6LgagBLcQQyAymbz","type":"tool_result","content":"# Summary: Russian mobile whitelist bypass (GitHub net4people/bbs #650, Aug 19 to Nov 2, 2026)\n\nThe thread is anecdotal. Most results come from individual testers, with no controlled, repeatable methodology beyond one pilot. Only Vadim Tolmachev's tests (Oct 8) are systematic.\n\n**Original post (Aug 19, amigocarra)**\n- VLESS+Reality with uTLS and decoy SNI on popular domains. Works under normal DPI blocking on MTS for one user, with thousands of logged connections.\n- A second MTS user (Moscow) could not connect at all through a German VPS or two Yandex Cloud IPs, even when the whitelist was apparently inactive.\n- A Yandex Cloud /22 appears in community CIDR lists and completes a Reality handshake, but has not been confirmed to work on a mobile device during an active whitelist.\n- Some tests showed that even whitelisted sites like gosuslugi.ru were unreachable, which does not match a simple default-deny CIDR+SNI model.\n\n**Comments**\n- **darkk (Aug 19):** On MTS in St. Petersburg, behavior changed after toggling airplane mode. `yandex.ru` switched from IPv6-only to IPv4-only reachability. Availability also varied by district, with `ya.ru` working in some areas and not others.\n- **pidrilaebanaja (Aug 21):** Pointed to a 4pda forum thread and said there are several different whitelists.\n- **EzoDdos (Aug 22, Izhevsk):** MTS behavior varied over time, sometimes blocking even whitelisted sites. MegaFon was described as the strictest. VK and Beeline worked on their MegaFon line, but Yandex did not. A reported workaround was about one minute in airplane mode, then a normal config, a CIDR pack, and two servers (one Russian, one foreign). Reality+VLESS was reportedly seen and blocked in their region.\n- **Vadim Tolmachev (Oct 8):** The most data-driven comment (see below).\n- **Lysenar (Oct 10, Oct 12):** Argues that many failures come from foreign DNS resolvers (e.g., 8.8.8.8) and cached results. Suggests the \"blackout\" may come from base-station or channel switching, possibly throttling to around 64 kbps or 128 kbps. Also mentions a \"golden SIM\" anomaly. These are unverified hypotheses.\n- **starovojtovvadim872-dot (Nov 1):** Claimed to be an MTS employee and offered to discuss. No technical content was given. The issue was closed on Nov 2 after the OP shared contact details privately.\n\n**Vadim Tolmachev's measurements (Oct 8)**\n- **Public CIDR lists are unreliable.** In a June Beeline test with six SIMs, HLL (AS51115) and Timeweb (AS9123) each passed some blocks and failed others within the same ASN.\n- **Yandex Cloud is inconsistent.** Addresses failed on June 3, but by July 1 other services were using them for entry points, and one worked on his SIM.\n- **Mass IP reservation failed.** 700+ floating IPs across five regions of one cloud provider included none in a known-permitted /24.\n- **Transport and SNI.** In an August paired test (wi-fi as control, different operator), 4 of 28 endpoints passed over the SIM. All were Reality with a permitted Russian SNI. Plain WebSocket with a permitted Host header passed 0 of 8. Not every Reality config passed.\n- **Regulatory context.** A Kommersant report (Aug 31) says Mintsifry asked hosting providers, CDNs, and web-protection services to move allowlisted resources to separate subnets, so unapproved customers, including VPNs, cannot use them.\n- **State resolver.** 195.208.4.1 stayed reachable on two operators. Behavior during a full blackout is unknown.\n\n**Answers to the original questions**\n1. **Working setups during whitelist mode:** None confirmed on MegaFon, Beeline, or Tele2. Only Reality with permitted SNI passed on MTS in one test, and results were inconsistent.\n2. **Per-operator differences:** Likely, based on the June vs. August results, though Tolmachev says he cannot separate operator differences from regime tightening.\n3. **Total blackout:** Unexplained. Hypotheses include base-station/channel effects and TSPU bugs. None are verified.\n4. **Finding the right IP pool:** No reliable method. Public lists were wrong in both directions. Only same-day SIM testing against the specific /24 was considered trustworthy.\n\n**Tools and resources mentioned**\n- **allowlist-probe** (github.com/vadim-tolmachev/allowlist-probe): a pilot probe for testing endpoints from SIMs. Tolmachev offered to test a posted /22 across three operators.\n- **Karing:** a client app used in the Izhevsk tests.\n- **4pda forum thread** (showtopic=1110469): a Russian-language discussion of whitelist types.\n- **Keenetic 4G routers and managed modems:** suggested by Lysenar for controlling channels and base-station selection."}]},"uuid":"99370e32-e937-4f64-ae3a-f36e16535ccd","timestamp":"2026-11-08T23:14:01.531Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":488833,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Summary: Russian mobile whitelist bypass (GitHub net4people/bbs #650, Aug 19 to Nov 2, 2026)\n\nThe thread is anecdotal. Most results come from individual testers, with no controlled, repeatable methodology beyond one pilot. Only Vadim Tolmachev's tests (Oct 8) are systematic.\n\n**Original post (Aug 19, amigocarra)**\n- VLESS+Reality with uTLS and decoy SNI on popular domains. Works under normal DPI blocking on MTS for one user, with thousands of logged connections.\n- A second MTS user (Moscow) could not connect at all through a German VPS or two Yandex Cloud IPs, even when the whitelist was apparently inactive.\n- A Yandex Cloud /22 appears in community CIDR lists and completes a Reality handshake, but has not been confirmed to work on a mobile device during an active whitelist.\n- Some tests showed that even whitelisted sites like gosuslugi.ru were unreachable, which does not match a simple default-deny CIDR+SNI model.\n\n**Comments**\n- **darkk (Aug 19):** On MTS in St. Petersburg, behavior changed after toggling airplane mode. `yandex.ru` switched from IPv6-only to IPv4-only reachability. Availability also varied by district, with `ya.ru` working in some areas and not others.\n- **pidrilaebanaja (Aug 21):** Pointed to a 4pda forum thread and said there are several different whitelists.\n- **EzoDdos (Aug 22, Izhevsk):** MTS behavior varied over time, sometimes blocking even whitelisted sites. MegaFon was described as the strictest. VK and Beeline worked on their MegaFon line, but Yandex did not. A reported workaround was about one minute in airplane mode, then a normal config, a CIDR pack, and two servers (one Russian, one foreign). Reality+VLESS was reportedly seen and blocked in their region.\n- **Vadim Tolmachev (Oct 8):** The most data-driven comment (see below).\n- **Lysenar (Oct 10, Oct 12):** Argues that many failures come from foreign DNS resolvers (e.g., 8.8.8.8) and cached results. Suggests the \"blackout\" may come from base-station or channel switching, possibly throttling to around 64 kbps or 128 kbps. Also mentions a \"golden SIM\" anomaly. These are unverified hypotheses.\n- **starovojtovvadim872-dot (Nov 1):** Claimed to be an MTS employee and offered to discuss. No technical content was given. The issue was closed on Nov 2 after the OP shared contact details privately.\n\n**Vadim Tolmachev's measurements (Oct 8)**\n- **Public CIDR lists are unreliable.** In a June Beeline test with six SIMs, HLL (AS51115) and Timeweb (AS9123) each passed some blocks and failed others within the same ASN.\n- **Yandex Cloud is inconsistent.** Addresses failed on June 3, but by July 1 other services were using them for entry points, and one worked on his SIM.\n- **Mass IP reservation failed.** 700+ floating IPs across five regions of one cloud provider included none in a known-permitted /24.\n- **Transport and SNI.** In an August paired test (wi-fi as control, different operator), 4 of 28 endpoints passed over the SIM. All were Reality with a permitted Russian SNI. Plain WebSocket with a permitted Host header passed 0 of 8. Not every Reality config passed.\n- **Regulatory context.** A Kommersant report (Aug 31) says Mintsifry asked hosting providers, CDNs, and web-protection services to move allowlisted resources to separate subnets, so unapproved customers, including VPNs, cannot use them.\n- **State resolver.** 195.208.4.1 stayed reachable on two operators. Behavior during a full blackout is unknown.\n\n**Answers to the original questions**\n1. **Working setups during whitelist mode:** None confirmed on MegaFon, Beeline, or Tele2. Only Reality with permitted SNI passed on MTS in one test, and results were inconsistent.\n2. **Per-operator differences:** Likely, based on the June vs. August results, though Tolmachev says he cannot separate operator differences from regime tightening.\n3. **Total blackout:** Unexplained. Hypotheses include base-station/channel effects and TSPU bugs. None are verified.\n4. **Finding the right IP pool:** No reliable method. Public lists were wrong in both directions. Only same-day SIM testing against the specific /24 was considered trustworthy.\n\n**Tools and resources mentioned**\n- **allowlist-probe** (github.com/vadim-tolmachev/allowlist-probe): a pilot probe for testing endpoints from SIMs. Tolmachev offered to test a posted /22 across three operators.\n- **Karing:** a client app used in the Izhevsk tests.\n- **4pda forum thread** (showtopic=1110469): a Russian-language discussion of whitelist types.\n- **Keenetic 4G routers and managed modems:** suggested by Lysenar for controlling channels and base-station selection.","durationMs":17367,"url":"https://github.com/net4people/bbs/issues/650"},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"1260c233-a71f-493d-91fd-3dca7595e035","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","root":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","branch":"feat/desktop-tun","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":2,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":2},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},{"name":"upstream","host":"github.com","remote":"chen08209/FlClash","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c6c30ecc-a993-4f47-9044-ce7c17ad4cb0","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","sessionId":"1fd4ae39-09c4-4cf0-9bfe-fc8abaa281eb","version":"2.1.317","gitBranch":"feat/desktop-tun","slug":"deep-singing-taco"}
{"type":"last-prompt","lastPrompt":"давай подумай очень хорошо поищи в интернет не гит хабе реапзитори как сделать так чтобы интернет у нас работал при включенном белый списки в рф","leafUuid":"99370e32-e937-4f64-ae3a-f36e16535ccd","sessionId":"1fd4ae39-09c4-4cf0-9bfe-fc8abaa281eb"}
