{"type":"bridge-session","sessionId":"1fd4ae39-09c4-4cf0-9bfe-fc8abaa281eb","bridgeSessionId":"cse_01XkaM9Z6bHd6CGFE5W7nPA1","lastSequenceNum":5816,"ownerAccountUuid":"f907b165-4712-485f-a44b-7f7d54b74cf9","ownerOrganizationUuid":"77d6453f-aaee-4ad3-a7ea-34b17cbaf2e5"}
{"parentUuid":"595f906e-58fc-4535-821e-c13746b7d89b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"91d163fa-4c33-4181-a871-4269cd8b22ce","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VGvqXyffhwEyKmaQPkfQqc","type":"tool_result","content":"# Russian Mobile Whitelists: How They Work and the Six Bypass Methods\n\nThe source is a Habr article by owenewans, published April 25 (the year isn't in the text, but the UPD notes date it to 2026). The author scanned whitelisted IPs over Megafon mobile internet. Their own caveat is that masscan results may be off by 20-30%.\n\n## How the filtering works\n\n**Layer 3 (IP/CIDR):** Traffic to non-whitelisted IPs (Google, Telegram, Twitter in the tests) gets no response at all, not even ICMP. Packets are dropped before reaching DPI. For whitelisted IPs, only some ports pass: TCP 80, 443, and 22 worked. UDP was almost entirely blocked, including QUIC (UDP 443), DNS (UDP 53), and WireGuard (UDP 51820).\n\n**Layer 7 (SNI):** For whitelisted IPs, the TSPU inspects the SNI in the TLS ClientHello and resets connections that match its blocklist. The SNI blocklist is inconsistent across networks. For example, twitter.com failed through VK and MAX but passed through Yandex. Google passed on SNI because, per the author's theory, it is blocked only by IP.\n\n**Where the TSPU sits:** By comparing TTL values, the author concluded that fake \"responses\" to blocked IPs (like Telegram's) came from a device about two hops away, right behind the operator's first router, not from the real server.\n\n**DNS:** External resolvers (8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1, 77.88.8.8) timed out. Only Megafon's own resolver (10.233.58.133) worked.\n\n**Scope and variation:** The author scanned 80,600 Russian subnets from RIPE allocations. They found 63,126 whitelisted IPs across 2,557 ASNs, about 0.14% of roughly 46 million Russian addresses. Enforcement varies by region, district, and operator. Moscow is episodic (about 3 hours), St. Petersburg is rare, and most regions are 24/7.\n\n**Priority tiers (per the author's analysis):**\n1. max.ru is always whitelisted.\n2. vk.com, ya.ru, and state-significant infrastructure are almost always included.\n3. Banks, marketplaces, and other services are inconsistent. Sberbank was notably absent, while salutejazz.ru was present.\n\n**Notable IP ranges mentioned:**\n- Yandex.Cloud (AS200350): 12,906 IPs, about one in five whitelisted addresses\n- Timeweb (AS9123): 2,904\n- Rostelecom (AS12389): 2,312\n- VK (AS47764, AS47541): about 2,750 combined\n- Selectel (AS49505, AS50340): about 1,950 combined\n- Beget, REG.RU, CDNvideo, and others\n- Densest /24s: 185.71.67.0/24 (Storm Networks, 37.5%), 95.129.236.0/24 (DDoS-Guard, 34.4%), 87.240.166.0/24 and 87.240.167.0/24 (VK CDN, ~31.6%), 77.88.0.0/24 (Yandex, 31.6%), 31.31.198.0/24 (REG.RU, 30.1%)\n- Sample IPs: 77.88.55.242 (Yandex), 87.240.132.78 (VK), 217.20.147.1 (MAX)\n\nFiltering is done per CIDR, so an entire /24 is passed or blocked based on the ranges the author observed.\n\n## The six bypass methods\n\nThe article does not mention XHTTP, so I can't say anything about it from this source.\n\n**1. VLESS + Reality on a Russian VPS** (author's main recommendation)\n- Requirements: a VPS with an IP in the whitelist (Timeweb, Yandex.Cloud, or VK Cloud), a whitelisted SNI such as vk.com, ya.ru, or vklive.enotfast.com, and Reality with XTLS-Vision.\n- Example config uses `fp=chrome` and `sni=vklive.enotfast.com`.\n- Other whitelisted SNIs from the author's scan of 1,176 domains include storage.yandex.net, yastatic.net, userapi.com, vkuser.net, vkuservideo.ru, hosting.reg.ru, cdnvideo.ru, and okcdn.ru.\n- Risky SNIs: twitter.com, x.com, and youtube.com (reset through some IPs).\n- Providers: Timeweb (free IP reroll, slow to get a good IP), Yandex.Cloud (4000₽ grant, good whitelist odds, but IPs are quickly blocked), VK Cloud (long-lived IPs, but complex verification and high cost).\n- Cost: about 2000₽/month. The author rates it highly stable and fast.\n- uTLS fingerprinting matters. Reality alone isn't enough if the fingerprint is recognizable.\n\n**2. Yandex Cloud Functions**\n- A serverless function acts as a SOCKS5 proxy via functions.yandexcloud.net, which is whitelisted across providers.\n- Free tier: 1M calls, 100,000 GB-seconds, and 40,000 GHz-seconds per month.\n- Requires a card for verification. Performance is worse than a direct VPS.\n\n**3. Yandex API Gateway** (patched; the author says it was banned after the article)\n- A domain on Cloudflare (DNS-only mode) points to an API Gateway. Yandex Certificate Manager issues a Let's Encrypt certificate. The OpenAPI spec forwards requests to a VPS outside the whitelist.\n- Traffic reaches whitelisted Yandex IPs, which terminate TLS legitimately.\n\n**4. olcRTC: tunneling through video calls** (pre-alpha, author's project)\n- Tunnels through WebRTC SFUs of whitelisted services: telemost.yandex.ru, salutejazz.ru, and stream.wb.ru.\n- DataChannel works, up to about 10 MB/s. V8 Channel (~1 MB/s) and VideoChannel (~100 KB/s) are in development.\n- wbstream (Wildberries) was the most stable, because it has little traffic inspection.\n\n**5. xDNS: DNS tunneling**\n- Traffic is encoded in DNS queries that go through public resolvers to the user's own nameserver, then into Xray.\n- Works only where UDP:53 to external DNS is open. The article's Megafon tests showed that wasn't the case, so this depends on the operator.\n- Low speed. Suitable for text and SSH, not video.\n\n**6. TURN relay (VK/Yandex TURN servers)**\n- WireGuard is relayed through public TURN servers from VK or Yandex, using cacggghp/vk-turn-proxy as an implementation.\n- Quickly banned. Yandex restricted its TURN relays to Yandex IPs, and VK added captchas.\n- Update dated April 29, 2026 (00:15 MSK): VK has been patched to throttle to about 250 kbps.\n\n## What doesn't work\n\n- **QUIC/HTTP3:** UDP 443 is blocked, even on some home ISPs.\n- **ECH/ESNI:** Blocked by the TSPU. The author argues it wouldn't help anyway, since blocking happens at L3 by IP.\n- **Standard VPN on a foreign VPS:** The IP isn't whitelisted, so traffic never leaves the network.\n- **Non-whitelisted SNIs on whitelisted IPs:** twitter.com, x.com, and youtube.com get reset through some IPs.\n- **External DNS:** Blocked on Megafon.\n\nThe author notes that whitelists sit on top of regular blocking. Domestic TSPU rules and TLS fingerprint detection still apply to traffic that passes L3."}]},"uuid":"da3ec5ef-3e9d-4297-b41b-878619a0bdee","timestamp":"2026-11-08T23:14:06.048Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":235318,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Russian Mobile Whitelists: How They Work and the Six Bypass Methods\n\nThe source is a Habr article by owenewans, published April 25 (the year isn't in the text, but the UPD notes date it to 2026). The author scanned whitelisted IPs over Megafon mobile internet. Their own caveat is that masscan results may be off by 20-30%.\n\n## How the filtering works\n\n**Layer 3 (IP/CIDR):** Traffic to non-whitelisted IPs (Google, Telegram, Twitter in the tests) gets no response at all, not even ICMP. Packets are dropped before reaching DPI. For whitelisted IPs, only some ports pass: TCP 80, 443, and 22 worked. UDP was almost entirely blocked, including QUIC (UDP 443), DNS (UDP 53), and WireGuard (UDP 51820).\n\n**Layer 7 (SNI):** For whitelisted IPs, the TSPU inspects the SNI in the TLS ClientHello and resets connections that match its blocklist. The SNI blocklist is inconsistent across networks. For example, twitter.com failed through VK and MAX but passed through Yandex. Google passed on SNI because, per the author's theory, it is blocked only by IP.\n\n**Where the TSPU sits:** By comparing TTL values, the author concluded that fake \"responses\" to blocked IPs (like Telegram's) came from a device about two hops away, right behind the operator's first router, not from the real server.\n\n**DNS:** External resolvers (8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1, 77.88.8.8) timed out. Only Megafon's own resolver (10.233.58.133) worked.\n\n**Scope and variation:** The author scanned 80,600 Russian subnets from RIPE allocations. They found 63,126 whitelisted IPs across 2,557 ASNs, about 0.14% of roughly 46 million Russian addresses. Enforcement varies by region, district, and operator. Moscow is episodic (about 3 hours), St. Petersburg is rare, and most regions are 24/7.\n\n**Priority tiers (per the author's analysis):**\n1. max.ru is always whitelisted.\n2. vk.com, ya.ru, and state-significant infrastructure are almost always included.\n3. Banks, marketplaces, and other services are inconsistent. Sberbank was notably absent, while salutejazz.ru was present.\n\n**Notable IP ranges mentioned:**\n- Yandex.Cloud (AS200350): 12,906 IPs, about one in five whitelisted addresses\n- Timeweb (AS9123): 2,904\n- Rostelecom (AS12389): 2,312\n- VK (AS47764, AS47541): about 2,750 combined\n- Selectel (AS49505, AS50340): about 1,950 combined\n- Beget, REG.RU, CDNvideo, and others\n- Densest /24s: 185.71.67.0/24 (Storm Networks, 37.5%), 95.129.236.0/24 (DDoS-Guard, 34.4%), 87.240.166.0/24 and 87.240.167.0/24 (VK CDN, ~31.6%), 77.88.0.0/24 (Yandex, 31.6%), 31.31.198.0/24 (REG.RU, 30.1%)\n- Sample IPs: 77.88.55.242 (Yandex), 87.240.132.78 (VK), 217.20.147.1 (MAX)\n\nFiltering is done per CIDR, so an entire /24 is passed or blocked based on the ranges the author observed.\n\n## The six bypass methods\n\nThe article does not mention XHTTP, so I can't say anything about it from this source.\n\n**1. VLESS + Reality on a Russian VPS** (author's main recommendation)\n- Requirements: a VPS with an IP in the whitelist (Timeweb, Yandex.Cloud, or VK Cloud), a whitelisted SNI such as vk.com, ya.ru, or vklive.enotfast.com, and Reality with XTLS-Vision.\n- Example config uses `fp=chrome` and `sni=vklive.enotfast.com`.\n- Other whitelisted SNIs from the author's scan of 1,176 domains include storage.yandex.net, yastatic.net, userapi.com, vkuser.net, vkuservideo.ru, hosting.reg.ru, cdnvideo.ru, and okcdn.ru.\n- Risky SNIs: twitter.com, x.com, and youtube.com (reset through some IPs).\n- Providers: Timeweb (free IP reroll, slow to get a good IP), Yandex.Cloud (4000₽ grant, good whitelist odds, but IPs are quickly blocked), VK Cloud (long-lived IPs, but complex verification and high cost).\n- Cost: about 2000₽/month. The author rates it highly stable and fast.\n- uTLS fingerprinting matters. Reality alone isn't enough if the fingerprint is recognizable.\n\n**2. Yandex Cloud Functions**\n- A serverless function acts as a SOCKS5 proxy via functions.yandexcloud.net, which is whitelisted across providers.\n- Free tier: 1M calls, 100,000 GB-seconds, and 40,000 GHz-seconds per month.\n- Requires a card for verification. Performance is worse than a direct VPS.\n\n**3. Yandex API Gateway** (patched; the author says it was banned after the article)\n- A domain on Cloudflare (DNS-only mode) points to an API Gateway. Yandex Certificate Manager issues a Let's Encrypt certificate. The OpenAPI spec forwards requests to a VPS outside the whitelist.\n- Traffic reaches whitelisted Yandex IPs, which terminate TLS legitimately.\n\n**4. olcRTC: tunneling through video calls** (pre-alpha, author's project)\n- Tunnels through WebRTC SFUs of whitelisted services: telemost.yandex.ru, salutejazz.ru, and stream.wb.ru.\n- DataChannel works, up to about 10 MB/s. V8 Channel (~1 MB/s) and VideoChannel (~100 KB/s) are in development.\n- wbstream (Wildberries) was the most stable, because it has little traffic inspection.\n\n**5. xDNS: DNS tunneling**\n- Traffic is encoded in DNS queries that go through public resolvers to the user's own nameserver, then into Xray.\n- Works only where UDP:53 to external DNS is open. The article's Megafon tests showed that wasn't the case, so this depends on the operator.\n- Low speed. Suitable for text and SSH, not video.\n\n**6. TURN relay (VK/Yandex TURN servers)**\n- WireGuard is relayed through public TURN servers from VK or Yandex, using cacggghp/vk-turn-proxy as an implementation.\n- Quickly banned. Yandex restricted its TURN relays to Yandex IPs, and VK added captchas.\n- Update dated April 29, 2026 (00:15 MSK): VK has been patched to throttle to about 250 kbps.\n\n## What doesn't work\n\n- **QUIC/HTTP3:** UDP 443 is blocked, even on some home ISPs.\n- **ECH/ESNI:** Blocked by the TSPU. The author argues it wouldn't help anyway, since blocking happens at L3 by IP.\n- **Standard VPN on a foreign VPS:** The IP isn't whitelisted, so traffic never leaves the network.\n- **Non-whitelisted SNIs on whitelisted IPs:** twitter.com, x.com, and youtube.com get reset through some IPs.\n- **External DNS:** Blocked on Megafon.\n\nThe author notes that whitelists sit on top of regular blocking. Domestic TSPU rules and TLS fingerprint detection still apply to traffic that passes L3.","durationMs":21885,"url":"https://habr.com/ru/articles/1027276/"},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"1260c233-a71f-493d-91fd-3dca7595e035","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","root":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","branch":"feat/desktop-tun","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":2,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":2},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},{"name":"upstream","host":"github.com","remote":"chen08209/FlClash","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"595f906e-58fc-4535-821e-c13746b7d89b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/Sagen