{"parentUuid":"ad2a8652-9aaa-47ad-b266-5b3918f34c82","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"91d163fa-4c33-4181-a871-4269cd8b22ce","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VCpJhjhUsoUVAokzEaaraz","type":"tool_result","content":"The \"Методичка по построению VPN, осень 2026\" section isn't available in full. The page shows only a teaser, and the note \"Публикация была скрыта или удалена\" indicates the article was hidden or deleted. The page therefore doesn't contain the requested entry/exit details, IP-finding procedures, or rotation methods.\n\n**What the teaser says:**\n- Over six weeks in autumn 2026, four foreign VPN servers were blocked, and each one lasted less time than the last. The last three were blocked within five days.\n- The setups used VLESS over REALITY with self-steal SNI on the operator's own domain, plus XHTTP, across different providers, countries, and ASNs. The author says they were blocked anyway.\n- In early November, foreign server IPs reportedly lived from a few hours to 1–3 days, regardless of protocol. Changing IPs helps only briefly.\n\n**Not in the page content:** entry/exit architecture details beyond the main article, specific hosting providers or ASNs for the methodology, gRPC or other transport choices, costs, the operators tested, and any hosting-ban warnings from that article.\n\n**Related content from the main article (dated 28 May, 2026):**\n- It describes a ENTRY/EXIT chain, where a Russian entry node forwards traffic to a European or US exit node. It says whitelisted IPs were found only at two providers, Yandex and VK.\n- It reports that VK reclaimed several servers' IPs without notice, and that a hosting provider was ordered to restrict VPN services. The author also cites a Ministry of Digital Development guideline calling for detection modules in accredited apps.\n\nIf you need the methodology itself, the original page or an archived copy would be the place to check."}]},"uuid":"6fb29d68-8334-49de-a5f0-742d411e520a","timestamp":"2026-11-08T23:15:04.677Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":132367,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The \"Методичка по построению VPN, осень 2026\" section isn't available in full. The page shows only a teaser, and the note \"Публикация была скрыта или удалена\" indicates the article was hidden or deleted. The page therefore doesn't contain the requested entry/exit details, IP-finding procedures, or rotation methods.\n\n**What the teaser says:**\n- Over six weeks in autumn 2026, four foreign VPN servers were blocked, and each one lasted less time than the last. The last three were blocked within five days.\n- The setups used VLESS over REALITY with self-steal SNI on the operator's own domain, plus XHTTP, across different providers, countries, and ASNs. The author says they were blocked anyway.\n- In early November, foreign server IPs reportedly lived from a few hours to 1–3 days, regardless of protocol. Changing IPs helps only briefly.\n\n**Not in the page content:** entry/exit architecture details beyond the main article, specific hosting providers or ASNs for the methodology, gRPC or other transport choices, costs, the operators tested, and any hosting-ban warnings from that article.\n\n**Related content from the main article (dated 28 May, 2026):**\n- It describes a ENTRY/EXIT chain, where a Russian entry node forwards traffic to a European or US exit node. It says whitelisted IPs were found only at two providers, Yandex and VK.\n- It reports that VK reclaimed several servers' IPs without notice, and that a hosting provider was ordered to restrict VPN services. The author also cites a Ministry of Digital Development guideline calling for detection modules in accredited apps.\n\nIf you need the methodology itself, the original page or an archived copy would be the place to check.","durationMs":10189,"url":"https://habr.com/ru/articles/1040846/"},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"bb249548-a910-494e-9a8e-4f9182698e90","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","root":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","branch":"feat/desktop-tun","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":2,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":2},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},{"name":"upstream","host":"github.com","remote":"chen08209/FlClash","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ad2a8652-9aaa-47ad-b266-5b3918f34c82","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","sessionId":"1fd4ae39-09c4-4cf0-9bfe-fc8abaa281eb","version":"2.1.317","gitBranch":"feat/desktop-tun","slug":"deep-singing-taco"}
{"parentUuid":"03c618a5-3d5d-4a14-932a-a380c66f6357","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"91d163fa-4c33-4181-a871-4269cd8b22ce","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TdjbWDX5xQwR7eNuT95cuS","type":"tool_result","content":"**Source:** Habr article \"Гайд по обходу «белых списков»...\" by adlayers, published Jan 15 (the year isn't shown in the text). All claims below are the author's and are unverified.\n\n**Why the chain:** The author says direct VLESS + Reality connections to European nodes are \"почти у всех под шейпингом.\" He claims TSPU now freezes sessions after about 15-20 KB per TCP connection instead of resetting them. Traffic from a Russian relay looks like ordinary inter-server traffic and is filtered less.\n\n**Required software:** Xray-core, minimum 25.12.8. The author also says to use an up-to-date core so Reality isn't detected through TLS 1.3 NewSessionTicket analysis.\n\n**Foreign (exit) node:**\n- VLESS inbound with the xhttp transport in packet-up mode. The exact config is only in an image, not in the text.\n- The article gives no SNI for this node.\n\n**Russian bridge node:**\n- The article doesn't show the bridge's inbound config. It only references an inbound tagged \"inbound-от-клиента\" that receives client traffic.\n- Outbound \"chain-to-europe\" uses protocol VLESS with a vnext entry pointing to the foreign node's IP on port 443.\n- Settings: flow xtls-rprx-vision, encryption none, transport xhttp, security reality, fingerprint chrome, serverName (SNI) \"vkvideo.ru\", xhttp mode packet-up, path \"/api/v1/update\".\n- Placeholders are used for the UUID, public key, and shortId.\n- A \"freedom\" outbound tagged DIRECT is also defined.\n\n**Routing (bridge):**\n- domainStrategy \"IPIfNonMatch\".\n- Send to DIRECT: geosite:category-ru, regexp for .ru domains, geosite:yandex, and full:cp.cloudflare.com (the author says this fixes endless timeouts on clients).\n- Send everything else from the client inbound to chain-to-europe.\n\n**Recommended Russian hosts (per the author):**\n- Yandex Cloud: most reliable, but you need to find good IP ranges.\n- VK Cloud: free traffic, but clean \"white\" IPs are nearly impossible to get.\n- EDGE: a decent alternative with CDN.\n\n**Checking whether an IP is whitelisted:** Run a web server on port 443 on the IP. If the site loads from a mobile connection (MTS or Megafon) without a VPN, the author considers the IP \"white.\"\n\n**What works under mobile whitelists (author's claims):**\n- The bridge IP must itself be whitelisted, or the chain won't work.\n- On iPhone, the VPN process has a roughly 50 MiB memory limit. Large Geo files can crash the client, so the author suggests packet-up instead of stream-up and lighter Geo files.\n- WhatsApp may hang while Instagram works. Suggested workarounds are adding IPv6 on the exit node or removing the vision flow for those services.\n- Services mentioned: hynet.cloud is described as the most universal and works on most large providers. DuckVPN reportedly has accessibility problems on MTS and Megafon. The other services (Amnezia, Voxiproxy, MamontVPN, SayVPN) are mostly described with caveats about platform focus or complexity.\n\n**Author's closing note:** He says chains are currently the only working method for passing whitelists, that finding clean cloud IPs is a lottery, and he recommends the Remnawave panel for managing chains. This reads as promotional."}]},"uuid":"1c4a6902-0b98-45c0-af7d-a8f575a50f3b","timestamp":"2026-11-08T23:15:06.000Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":102915,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Source:** Habr article \"Гайд по обходу «белых списков»...\" by adlayers, published Jan 15 (the year isn't shown in the text). All claims below are the author's and are unverified.\n\n**Why the chain:** The author says direct VLESS + Reality connections to European nodes are \"почти у всех под шейпингом.\" He claims TSPU now freezes sessions after about 15-20 KB per TCP connection instead of resetting them. Traffic from a Russian relay looks like ordinary inter-server traffic and is filtered less.\n\n**Required software:** Xray-core, minimum 25.12.8. The author also says to use an up-to-date core so Reality isn't detected through TLS 1.3 NewSessionTicket analysis.\n\n**Foreign (exit) node:**\n- VLESS inbound with the xhttp transport in packet-up mode. The exact config is only in an image, not in the text.\n- The article gives no SNI for this node.\n\n**Russian bridge node:**\n- The article doesn't show the bridge's inbound config. It only references an inbound tagged \"inbound-от-клиента\" that receives client traffic.\n- Outbound \"chain-to-europe\" uses protocol VLESS with a vnext entry pointing to the foreign node's IP on port 443.\n- Settings: flow xtls-rprx-vision, encryption none, transport xhttp, security reality, fingerprint chrome, serverName (SNI) \"vkvideo.ru\", xhttp mode packet-up, path \"/api/v1/update\".\n- Placeholders are used for the UUID, public key, and shortId.\n- A \"freedom\" outbound tagged DIRECT is also defined.\n\n**Routing (bridge):**\n- domainStrategy \"IPIfNonMatch\".\n- Send to DIRECT: geosite:category-ru, regexp for .ru domains, geosite:yandex, and full:cp.cloudflare.com (the author says this fixes endless timeouts on clients).\n- Send everything else from the client inbound to chain-to-europe.\n\n**Recommended Russian hosts (per the author):**\n- Yandex Cloud: most reliable, but you need to find good IP ranges.\n- VK Cloud: free traffic, but clean \"white\" IPs are nearly impossible to get.\n- EDGE: a decent alternative with CDN.\n\n**Checking whether an IP is whitelisted:** Run a web server on port 443 on the IP. If the site loads from a mobile connection (MTS or Megafon) without a VPN, the author considers the IP \"white.\"\n\n**What works under mobile whitelists (author's claims):**\n- The bridge IP must itself be whitelisted, or the chain won't work.\n- On iPhone, the VPN process has a roughly 50 MiB memory limit. Large Geo files can crash the client, so the author suggests packet-up instead of stream-up and lighter Geo files.\n- WhatsApp may hang while Instagram works. Suggested workarounds are adding IPv6 on the exit node or removing the vision flow for those services.\n- Services mentioned: hynet.cloud is described as the most universal and works on most large providers. DuckVPN reportedly has accessibility problems on MTS and Megafon. The other services (Amnezia, Voxiproxy, MamontVPN, SayVPN) are mostly described with caveats about platform focus or complexity.\n\n**Author's closing note:** He says chains are currently the only working method for passing whitelists, that finding clean cloud IPs is a lottery, and he recommends the Remnawave panel for managing chains. This reads as promotional.","durationMs":11513,"url":"https://habr.com/en/articles/985674/"},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"bb249548-a910-494e-9a8e-4f9182698e90","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","root":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","branch":"feat/desktop-tun","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":2,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":2},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"},{"name":"upstream","host":"github.com","remote":"chen08209/FlClash","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Shahen777/nexo-app","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"03c618a5-3d5d-4a14-932a-a380c66f6357","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Projects/nexo-app","sessionId":"1fd4ae39-09c4-4